<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babylon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Babylon was an ancient city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and one of the most famous cities of the ancient world. It was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>'s Babylonian Empire and later of the Chaldean or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>. It was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> in 539 BCE and remained an important centre under the Achaemenid and Seleucid empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babylon.jpg" alt="Babylon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Stanley · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ishtar_Gate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Babylon stood on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, within modern Hillah, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, about 85 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. It was the main cultural and political centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, the Akkadian-speaking region. The name comes from the Akkadian Bābilim, usually explained as &quot;gate of the god(s)&quot;, though this is increasingly seen as a folk etymology for an older name of unknown origin. It has been estimated that Babylon was the largest city in the world c. 1770 to c. 1670 BCE and again c. 612 to c. 320 BCE. Excavation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> texts, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and classical authors give an incomplete and sometimes contradictory picture of it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest mention is from the reign of the Akkadian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shar-kali-sharri">Shar-Kali-Sharri</a> (2217 to 2193 BCE), when Babylon was a town governed by an appointed governor. Amorite rulers made it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> (r. 1792 to 1750 BCE) conquered the southern cities and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, ruling them from Babylon. In 1595 BCE the city is thought to have been sacked by the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mursili-i">Mursili I</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassite-dynasty">Kassite dynasty</a> then ruled for 435 years, until 1160 BCE. In 689 BCE Sennacherib of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> razed Babylon after repeated revolts, and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> rebuilt it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabopolassar">Nabopolassar</a> freed the city from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, and the allied Medo-Babylonian armies destroyed the Assyrian Empire between 626 and 609 BCE. Nebuchadnezzar II (604 to 561 BCE) rebuilt the imperial grounds, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etemenanki">Etemenanki</a> ziggurat and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanging-gardens-of-babylon">Hanging Gardens</a> are credited to him, but scholars dispute whether they existed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> took the city after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Gaugamela</a> in 331 BCE and died there in 323 BCE, and Babylon then belonged to the Seleucid, Parthian and Sasanian empires. The last known habitation, from the 11th century CE, calls it the small village of Babel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Claudius Rich excavated the site in 1811–12, and the German Oriental Society dug there from 1899 to 1917. Replicas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate</a> and the Ninmakh Temple were built on the site in the 1960s, while a reconstruction of the gate stands in the Pergamon Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. UNESCO recognised Babylon as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2019.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Babylon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 539 BCE Cyrus defeated the Babylonian army at the Battle of Opis and entered <b>Babylon</b>, absorbing the Neo-Babylonian Empire with little resistance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Middle Assyrian kingdom then emerged under Ashur-uballit I (r. c. 1365–1330 BCE), who used the Hittite defeat of Mitanni to assert independence and claim equality with the great kings of Egypt and <b>Babylon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1894 BCE an Amorite chieftain named Sumu-abum (Sumuabum) took control of <b>Babylon</b>, traditionally from the neighbouring city-state of Kazallu, and began building a defensive wall around the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Nabopolassar, a leader of uncertain origin traditionally regarded as a Chaldean chieftain, seized <b>Babylon</b> in 626 BCE amid the turmoil that followed the death of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kassite kings of <b>Babylon</b> placed a governor on the island in the fifteenth and fourteenth centuries BCE, and the Middle Assyrian king Tukulti-Ninurta I (r. c. 1243–1207 BCE) styled himself king of Dilmun and Meluhha, a claim probably more boastful than real.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Known from Egyptian, Assyrian and biblical sources, it fell to <b>Babylon</b> in the 6th century BCE, and its people lived on in southern Judah as the Idumaeans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
